<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Graces</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charites, or Graces, were goddesses of grace, beauty and festivity in Greek religion, usually three in number: Aglaia, Euphrosyne and Thalia. Their oldest and most famous cult was at Orchomenus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where they were worshipped as stones said to have fallen from the sky and honoured with the Charitesia festival of music and poetry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>'s Fourteenth Olympian ode celebrates them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charites.jpg" alt="Charites" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Three_Graces,_from_Pompeii_(fresco).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
